**Claim:** $e(F \circ f) = e(f)$.

**Setup and notation.** Let $(M, g)$, $(N, h)$, $(Q, q)$ be pseudo-Riemannian manifolds. The energy density of a smooth map $f:(M,g)\to(N,h)$ is defined pointwise by

$$e(f)(x) = \tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathrm{tr}_g\!\big(f^* h\big)_x = \tfrac{1}{2}\,g^{ij}(x)\,h_{f(x)}\!\big(df_x(\partial_i),\, df_x(\partial_j)\big),$$

where $\{g^{ij}\}$ is the inverse of the matrix of $g$ in local coordinates $\{x^i\}$ on $M$, and $\{\partial_i\}$ are the corresponding coordinate vectors.

**Using the immersion hypothesis.** We are given that $F:(N,h)\to(Q,q)$ is a pseudo-Riemannian immersion, i.e., $h = F^* q$. By definition of the pullback metric, for any $y\in N$ and any $X, Y \in T_y N$,

$$h_y(X, Y) = (F^* q)_y(X, Y) = q_{F(y)}\!\big(dF_y(X),\, dF_y(Y)\big).$$

**Computing $e(F\circ f)$.** The composite $F\circ f : (M,g) \to (Q,q)$ has energy density

$$e(F\circ f)(x) = \tfrac{1}{2}\,g^{ij}(x)\,q_{F(f(x))}\!\big(d(F\circ f)_x(\partial_i),\, d(F\circ f)_x(\partial_j)\big).$$

By the chain rule, $d(F\circ f)_x = dF_{f(x)} \circ df_x$, so

$$d(F\circ f)_x(\partial_i) = dF_{f(x)}\!\big(df_x(\partial_i)\big).$$

Substituting,

$$e(F\circ f)(x) = \tfrac{1}{2}\,g^{ij}(x)\,q_{F(f(x))}\!\big(dF_{f(x)}(df_x(\partial_i)),\, dF_{f(x)}(df_x(\partial_j))\big).$$

Now apply the immersion identity $h = F^*q$ with $y = f(x)$, $X = df_x(\partial_i)$, $Y = df_x(\partial_j)$:

$$q_{F(f(x))}\!\big(dF_{f(x)}(df_x(\partial_i)),\, dF_{f(x)}(df_x(\partial_j))\big) = h_{f(x)}\!\big(df_x(\partial_i),\, df_x(\partial_j)\big).$$

Therefore

$$e(F\circ f)(x) = \tfrac{1}{2}\,g^{ij}(x)\,h_{f(x)}\!\big(df_x(\partial_i),\, df_x(\partial_j)\big) = e(f)(x).$$

**Conclusion.** Since this holds at every $x\in M$,

$$\boxed{e(F\circ f) = e(f).}$$

The energy density is invariant under post-composition with a pseudo-Riemannian immersion. Intuitively, because $h$ is *defined* as the pullback of $q$ through $F$, the map $F$ embeds $(N,h)$ isometrically (in the pseudo-Riemannian sense) into $(Q,q)$, so measuring the "stretching" of $f$ using $h$ on the target is identical to measuring the stretching of $F\circ f$ using $q$.

